Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 15:03:58 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 043/154] feat: Cmd+Opt+Down and Up walk the sidebar's order
 again
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Axis 1 of tab cycling. Those two combos have been completely inert since the
Swift cut - no stock command owns them - because the order they walked lived
in the Swift model.

The sidebar's order is not the strip's, which is the whole reason this axis
exists beside stock Ctrl-Tab: a space keeps rows for tabs that are not open,
and the user reorders rows by dragging. BuildSidebarTabsJSON is where that
order surfaces now, so the walk reads it and flattens it in DRAWN order -
pinned, then each folder's rows, then the ordinary tabs. Favourites are tiles
rather than rows, and a favourite is not a tab, so they are not in the cycle.

The row choice is a pure unit with nine cases, because each of its decisions
is a thing a reader assumes rather than checks:

  A single row is not a cycle. Returning it re-activates the tab the user is
  already on, which looks like the key doing nothing while it did something.

  Nothing selected enters the list from the end the walk is heading towards.
  The active tab is not always a row - a favourite-bound tab is never
  persisted into the space, and a space switch leaves nothing marked
  mid-flight - so refusing would make the key dead in exactly the cases where
  the user is most likely to press it.

  A cold row is a target. It is on screen, so the cycle must reach it; it has
  no id, so its URL goes through OpenSidebarColdTab, the same funnel a click
  uses and the one holding the scheme and realised-window checks.

Axis 0, Ctrl-Tab, is NOT done and is not pretended to be. It needs a recency
list nothing here keeps, and unlike axis 1 it is not inert: it falls through
to stock strip cycling, which is a reasonable meaning to leave in place. The
detector and early return that make that fall-through work are untouched.

Signed-off-by: János Mikó <693558+janosmiko@users.noreply.github.com>
